## 7. Automation Framework Design

The framework follows the Page Object Model design pattern.

- **Page Objects:** Store application locators and reusable actions.
- **Test Files:** Contain test scenarios and assertions.
- **Test Data:** Maintained separately in JSON files.
- **Configuration:** Manages the base URL, browser settings and test credentials.
- **Assertions:** Validate actual results against expected business requirements.

Each test is designed to execute independently without relying on the execution order of other tests.

Playwright's built-in auto-waiting and locator assertions are used instead of fixed delays.

## 8. Test Reporting and Failure Handling

The framework generates an HTML report and JUnit XML report.

Screenshots are automatically captured when a test fails using:

```typescript
screenshot: 'only-on-failure'
```

Playwright traces are retained on failure to support debugging.

**Generate the HTML report:**

```bash
npx playwright test
npx playwright show-report
```

## 9. Scaling the Automation Framework to 200 Test Cases

To scale this framework to 200 automated test cases, I would organize the tests into separate functional modules such as Login, Products, Cart, Checkout, and Security. I would continue using the Page Object Model to maintain reusable locators and methods, reducing code duplication and improving maintainability. Test data would be managed through JSON files and parameterized tests, while Playwright fixtures would handle common setup and teardown operations. Independent tests could execute in parallel to reduce execution time. I would also integrate the automation suite with a CI/CD pipeline using GitHub Actions or Jenkins, maintain HTML and JUnit reports, and use screenshots and traces to investigate failures. Each automated test would retain its corresponding manual test case ID to ensure traceability as the suite grows.

## 10. Conclusion

This automation project demonstrates the implementation of Playwright with TypeScript and the Page Object Model to validate critical e-commerce business workflows.

The framework focuses on reusable code, independent test execution, data-driven testing, reliable assertions, reporting, and maintainability.
 